                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "msc2":</h3><p>The <b>Hiller MSC-2</b> was an early model of vacuum tube, designed in Germany by Albert Hiller. It was a triode tube, running on 4V.</p><p><img src="/images/tubes/MSC2-tube.jpg" width="100" alt="Hiller MSC-2 triode tube" class="thumb shaft" />Neumann used it in several of its early microphone designs, including for a time the famous Neumann bottle microphone.</p><p>In most mic applications, the MSC-2 was replaced by the Telefunken AC701, which was slightly smaller.</p><p>Read more about the <a href="http://www.radiomuseum.org/tubes/tube_msc2.html">Hiller MSC-2</a>.</p>
